(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-14"] = {"14.1":{"s":"Light behaves as a wave in interference, diffraction and polarisation. But black body radiation and the photoelectric effect cannot be explained that way. Planck proposed that energy is given out in packets (quanta) of size hν, so higher frequency means bigger packets. This chapter shows that radiation and matter both have a wave side and a particle side.","f":[{"x":"E = n*h*nu","n":"Planck: an oscillator gives out energy only in whole packets of h nu; n = 1, 2, 3 ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.1, p.306"}]},"14.2":{"s":"Light of high enough frequency knocks electrons out of a metal surface. Emission starts at once, needs a frequency above a threshold that depends on the metal, and the electrons' greatest kinetic energy rises with frequency but not with intensity; intensity only changes the current. Wave theory cannot explain this. Einstein: light comes as photons of energy hν; one photon gives all its energy to one electron, which spends the work function to escape.","f":[{"x":"E = h*nu = h*c/lambda","n":"Energy of one photon; h = Planck's constant = 6.63 × 10^-34 J s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.311"},{"x":"nu_0 = phi_0/h","n":"Threshold frequency; phi_0 = work function of the metal.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.311"},{"x":"KE_max = h*nu - phi_0","n":"Einstein's photoelectric equation.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.312"},{"x":"e*V_0 = h*nu - phi_0","n":"V_0 = stopping potential: the reverse voltage that just stops the fastest electrons.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.313"},{"x":"V_0 = (h/e)*nu - phi_0/e","n":"Stopping potential against frequency is a straight line of slope h/e, the same for every metal.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.2, p.313"}],"w":["Brighter light gives more electrons (more current), not faster ones.","Below the threshold frequency no electrons come out, however bright the light."]},"14.3":{"s":"Light shows wave behaviour (interference, diffraction) in some experiments and particle behaviour (photoelectric effect, Compton scattering) in others; we need both pictures. Compton found that X-rays scattered by electrons come out with a longer wavelength, as in a collision of particles: photons carry momentum as well as energy. A photon always moves at the speed of light and has zero rest mass.","f":[{"x":"Delta lambda = (h/(m_0*c))*(1 - cos(theta))","n":"Compton shift; theta = scattering angle, m_0 = electron's rest mass. Does not depend on the incident wavelength.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.3, p.315"}],"w":["In photon collisions energy and momentum are conserved, but the number of photons is not."]},"14.4":{"s":"A photocell uses the photoelectric effect to turn light into an electric current. A curved photosensitive cathode and a wire anode sit in an evacuated glass or quartz bulb with a battery and a microammeter. When suitable light falls on the cathode, a current flows; when the light is cut off, it stops. Uses: light meters, automatic street lights, burglar alarms and counters.","w":["A solar cell is not a photocell of this kind: it makes a voltage in a semiconductor (photovoltaic effect)."]},"14.5":{"s":"De Broglie proposed that, since radiation behaves as both wave and particle, moving matter should too. A particle with momentum p has a matter wave of wavelength h/p. The larger the momentum, the shorter the wavelength. For a charged particle accelerated from rest through a potential difference V, its kinetic energy is qV, which gives the wavelength directly.","f":[{"x":"p = E/c = h/lambda","n":"Momentum of a photon.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"},{"x":"lambda = h/(m*v)","n":"De Broglie wavelength of a particle of mass m and speed v.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"},{"x":"lambda = h/sqrt(2*m*q*V)","n":"Charged particle q accelerated from rest through V (not near light speed).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"},{"x":"lambda = 1.228/sqrt(V) nm","n":"Electron accelerated through V volts.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 14.5, p.317"}],"w":["p = E/c is only for photons; for an electron use p = mv or the kinetic energy.","For the same kinetic energy a heavier particle has a shorter wavelength."]},"14.6":{"s":"Davisson and Germer fired electrons from an electron gun at a nickel crystal and measured how many were scattered at each angle. Instead of an even spread they found a strong peak, at 50° for 54 V electrons, caused by interference of waves scattered from layers of atoms. The measured wavelength (about 0.165 nm) agreed with de Broglie's 1.228/√54 ≈ 0.167 nm, proving that electrons behave as waves.","w":["Electron wavelengths are a few ångström, the size of atomic spacing, which is why a crystal works as the grating."]},"14.7":{"s":"All moving matter has a wave nature, just as radiation has a particle nature. The wave side only shows up for very small objects, because h is tiny: a running child or a moving car has a de Broglie wavelength far smaller than anything around it, so no diffraction is seen. For electrons and atoms the wavelength matches atomic sizes and wave effects become important.","w":["Everyday objects do have a wavelength; it is just far too small to notice (about 10⁻³⁴ m or less; 6.6 × 10⁻³⁶ m for a running student)."]}};
